{"kind":"artifact","text":"The internal/service directory's reviewed head differs from main through two generated files nested under it, and through nothing else. Exact clean pushed head 1b453fea2ce30b1d2d6d773234d939c9cd79d6b1 on request/lifecycle-none-rendering, unchanged; tree 22f4abc5ee9abf6cc5aae66c64892a386b1c51fb. Under planner's corrected request b7310bf4 and my promise dad7e9e5. Published on codex's ruling 233a3dc4 and planner's 849a94ae, which established that this artifact is required rather than optional.\n\nWHAT THIS ARTIFACT CLAIMS, AND EQUALLY WHAT IT DOES NOT. Between ebdca9fa and this head, the files changed under internal/service are exactly two, and I listed them rather than recalling them: internal/service/uidist/assets/index-C3Bib4Qr.js and internal/service/uidist/index.html. Filtering the same diff for Go source returns nothing. So this artifact does NOT claim that any internal/service/*.go changed, and it does not claim review coverage of the Go service. It says the narrower true thing: the directory's state at this reviewed head differs from main through the nested generated browser bundle, which is what makes the wider path a covering predecessor of this merge.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS, SINCE I ARGUED AGAINST IT AN HOUR AGO AND WAS WRONG. When gs merge refused this merge for pulling the parent path into the retirement set, I read the refusal as a scoping defect and said so. Codex corrected me at 233a3dc4 and the correction is right. The exact-string rule and the coverage rule are different rules: internal/service never becomes the same artifact path as internal/service/uidist for provenance identity, but every live artifact covering a changed file must still be accounted for, and where a directory and something inside it are both live over one changed file the wider path wins. The refusal was the cross-author guard reading reviewed reach downward only — a review of internal/service/uidist cannot authorize retiring another actor's internal/service pointer — and that asymmetry is a safety property, not an oversight. Publishing this artifact is the honest way through, because it makes the coverage claim explicitly and puts it in front of a reviewer, rather than widening an authorization around an approval that never cited the path.\n\nWHAT IS LIVE AT THIS PATH, all four stale, authors checked: 8b1080a3 at 95374ec2 and 1580c3aa at 7da8b753 are mine; a4666bc2 at e9b41a66 and 6079ad12 at ebdca9fa are codex's, the last published by their completion merge. I am not retiring any of them here and I did not retire any of them earlier when doing so would have unblocked me. They are for gs merge to account for atomically once a reviewer has signed this path.\n\nTHE SUBSTANCE UNDER REVIEW IS UNCHANGED AND LIVES ELSEWHERE. The behaviour of this head is in ui/src/lib and is described by artifact 6e5344bf at ui; the byte-reproduction of the bundle is described by 204cf2f7 at internal/service/uidist. Both are live and unstale at filing and both sit at this same head. This artifact adds a third path to the coverage set; it adds no new claim about behaviour. Codex has already approved this exact head once at 912e27de, which I ratified at e8112bb2, and the tree has not moved since — the reason a fresh verdict is needed is that the earlier one signed two paths and the merge requires three.\n\nREPRODUCIBILITY AT THIS HEAD: make ui leaves git status --porcelain empty and make ui-check exits 0, so the two generated files are exactly what the source at this head produces. No file under internal/service was hand-edited.\n\nGATES at this exact head, run by me alone after an earlier concurrent run produced load-induced failures: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 34 ok and zero FAIL; npm test 136 pass 0 fail exit 0; gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"1b453fea2ce30b1d2d6d773234d939c9cd79d6b1","path":"internal/service"}}